\description{
Build an Ensembl Compara orthology snapshot for a set of species from their gene sets, the gene trees and every species' homology dump, and optionally write it as Parquet and say, gene by gene, how far Compara's inference got in another species.

Answers \strong{which mouse genes are orthologous to this human gene, and if none, why not?} Wraps
mzLib's \code{OrthologySnapshot.Build} and \code{OrthologySnapshotWriter.WriteAsync} (mzLib #1381): which
genes of one species are orthologous to which genes of another, kept exactly as Compara states
it. One-to-many and many-to-many orthologies are several rows, paralogs are kept, and nothing is
picked, merged or collapsed. Local files only; nothing is downloaded.
}
\details{
\strong{Every species' homology dump is required, and mzLib refuses a call without one.} Compara puts
each of a pair's relationships in one of the pair's two dumps, arbitrarily, and in practice they
partition: in release 116 every human-mouse orthology is in the *mouse* dump and none is in the
human one. Reading the dump named after your species gives a complete-looking, badly incomplete
answer.

\strong{Orthology is pairwise and not transitive.} A~B and B~C do not make A~C, so never chain pairs
into groups. The written \code{views.sql} has a \code{species_set3} macro that forms a tuple only when
every two members are orthologs.

The relationships are in the Parquet files \code{out} writes, which mzLib cannot read back and mzLibR
does not read (it has no dependencies): query them with the \code{arrow} or \code{duckdb} packages, as the
proteins article shows. Without \code{out}, the call checks that the inputs fit together and, with
\code{pair_status}, says gene by gene how far Compara's inference got in one other species.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_orthology_snapshot}. The relationships themselves are in the Parquet files
  \code{out} writes, not here: this says what went in, what came out, and, with \code{pair_status}, every
  gene's status in one other species.

  \code{release} is the release you gave; \code{species} the snapshot's species, in sorted order; \code{format}
  is \code{"ensembl-orthology-snapshot"} and \code{format_version} its version, as \code{manifest.json} names
  them. \code{snapshot_id} is mzLib's id for the snapshot, a sha256 over the format, the release, the
  species and every input's sha256: the same inputs give the same id, written or not. Record it
  with any result drawn from the snapshot. \code{identical_duplicates_dropped} counts homology rows
  found in both of a pair's dumps, identical, and counted once, in rows: 0 in release 116, whose
  dumps partition.

  \code{gene_sets} is a data.frame, one row per species: \code{species}, \code{source_file_name}, \code{sha256} (the
  value \code{\link{proteins_resolve_genes}} reports as \code{gene_set_sha256}, so a resolution and a snapshot
  can be checked against one gene set), \code{release}, \code{genome_build} and \code{genebuild_last_updated}
  (\code{NA} when the GTF's name or headers carry none), and \code{gene_count}, in genes, every biotype.

  \code{gene_trees} is a list: \code{source_file_name}, \code{sha256}, \code{release} (\code{NA} when the name carries
  none, so it was not checked), \code{collection} (e.g. \code{"vertebrates"}), \code{member_count}, the genes
  held from the file, and \code{restricted_to_gene_sets}, the gene sets' sha256s the members were
  restricted to, or \code{NULL} when every gene in the file was kept.

  \code{dumps} is a data.frame, one row per species, the species read from the dump's rows:
  \code{source_file_name} (the same for every species, so it alone does not say whose dump it is),
  \code{sha256}, \code{release}, \code{rows_read}, every data row read and checked, in rows, and \code{rows_kept}, the
  rows whose two species are both snapshot species, in rows.

  \code{pairs} is a data.frame, one row per species pair \code{(a, b)} with \code{a} sorting first, \code{(s, s)}
  included: \code{species_a}, \code{species_b}, and the relationships between them in rows - \code{row_count},
  one row each, never merged or picked; \code{ortholog_count}, always 0 for \code{(s, s)};
  \code{paralog_count}, always 0 between two species; and \code{homoeolog_count}. See
  \code{\link{proteins_orthology_pair}}.

  \code{written} is \code{NULL} without \code{out}. With it, a list: \code{directory}, absolute; \code{manifest}, the path
  of \code{manifest.json}, which lists every input and file with its sha256; and \code{files}, a data.frame
  of every file written but the manifest, in order of path: \code{path} (relative to \code{directory}, with
  forward slashes), \code{rows} (rows in the Parquet table, \code{NA} for \code{views.sql}, 0 for a pair with no
  relationship, which is still written), \code{bytes} (the file's size in bytes) and \code{sha256}.

  \code{pair_status} is \code{NULL} without \code{pair_status}. With it, a list: \code{from_species}, \code{to_species},
  \code{row_count} (every gene of \code{from_species}, in genes), \code{status_counts} (a named vector, status to
  genes with it, every status present in mzLib's order, zeros included), \code{column_names}, and
  \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per gene in order of gene id: \code{gene_id}, \code{gene_name} (\code{NA}
  when the GTF gives none: a label, never a key), \code{gene_biotype} and \code{status}. \code{caveats} names this
  call's traps.

}
\section{What each status means}{


It says how far Compara's inference got, not what biology holds. \code{has_ortholog}: at least one
ortholog in \code{to_species}. \code{no_edge_in_shared_tree}: the gene's tree holds a \code{to_species} gene,
and Compara called no ortholog to it - the nearest the data comes to "no ortholog exists".
\code{tree_lacks_target_species}: the gene's tree holds no \code{to_species} gene, so nothing was
compared. \code{not_in_any_tree}: the gene is in the gene set but in no gene tree, so no call was
attempted. \strong{Every biotype is a row}, so choose your denominator by filtering on
\code{gene_biotype}. One status's genes are
\code{ps$records$gene_id[ps$records$status == "not_in_any_tree"]}, with \code{ps <- snap$pair_status}.

}
\section{The traps}{


\strong{The gene set and the dumps must be one release.} A homology naming a gene its gene set lacks
is refused as an \code{mzlib_bridge_error} (\code{InvalidDataException}): that is what a GTF and dumps from
different releases look like. Use the primary-assembly GTF, as for \code{\link{proteins_resolve_genes}}.
\strong{Keep Ensembl's file names}: mzLib holds the gene trees and the dumps to \code{release} only when
their names carry it, and a caveat says which were not checked. \strong{A directory that is not empty
is refused} as \code{out}: a snapshot is written once, and next release's goes in a new directory.

}
