\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{release}}{string; required; range \code{an Ensembl release number, e.g. 116}. The Ensembl release of the Compara inputs. Required, never defaulted: the release is part of the answer. mzLib holds the gene trees and every dump to it, but only when their file names carry a release (Compara.116.protein_default.homologies.tsv.gz, vertebrates.GeneTree_content.default.e116.txt.gz); a renamed file is not checked, and the result's caveats say which. A GTF's own number is not compared with it: Ensembl publishes some species with their own annotation release (Saccharomyces_cerevisiae.R64-1-1.63.gtf.gz in release 116), so mzLib holds a gene set to the dumps by content instead.}
\item{wire \code{--species}}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 line; each exactly three tab-separated fields}. Read from stdin (not an option), first: one line per species, the species' Ensembl production name (homo_sapiens, as the dumps' species column spells it), a tab, gtf or gene-set, a tab, and the path. gtf is an Ensembl primary-assembly GTF, plain or .gz, read with EnsemblGeneSet.LoadGtf; gene-set is the compact table mzLib's EnsemblGeneSetWriter makes from one, read with EnsemblGeneSetReader.Load, which keeps the GTF's provenance. These lines are the snapshot's species. A species listed twice, a kind other than gtf or gene-set, or a missing file is a usage error. Blank lines are skipped. \emph{Not an argument here: rendered from gtfs and gene_sets, two named character vectors from Ensembl production name to path, one stdin line per species.}}
\item{\code{dumps}}{string[]; required; range \code{exactly one per species}. Read from stdin (not an option), after a line holding only --: one Compara homology dump path per line (homologies/SPECIES/Compara.RELEASE.protein_default.homologies.tsv.gz, plain or .gz), each read with ComparaHomologyDump.Load for every species given. The dump's genome is read from its rows, not its name. mzLib requires EVERY species' dump: Compara puts each of a pair's relationships in one of the pair's two dumps, arbitrarily, so a missing dump would silently lose them. A missing dump, two of one species, or one of a species not given is mzLib's refusal, a usage error.}
\item{\code{gene_trees} (wire \code{--gene-trees})}{path; required. Compara's gene-tree membership dump, COLLECTION.GeneTree_content.default.eRELEASE.txt.gz (plain or .gz), read with ComparaGeneTreeContent.Load. It is what tells a gene whose tree holds no gene of the other species from one whose tree does but got no ortholog call.}
\item{\code{restrict_gene_trees} (wire \code{--restrict-gene-trees})}{flag; default \code{FALSE}. Keep only the gene-tree members in the given gene sets (ComparaGeneTreeContent.Load with restrictTo, the gene sets). Absent: mzLib's default, restrictTo null, which keeps every gene in the file, 4.2 million for the vertebrate collection of release 116 (mzLib's ComparaGeneTreeContent remarks). The snapshot, its id and every written byte are the same either way; only memory, gene_trees.member_count and gene_trees.restricted_to_gene_sets differ. mzLib checks the one-tree and one-canonical-protein rules only on the genes kept.}
\item{\code{out}}{path; default absent; range \code{a directory that does not exist or is empty}. Write the snapshot to this directory with OrthologySnapshotWriter.WriteAsync: Parquet tables (zstd) genes/SPECIES.parquet, members/SPECIES.parquet and pairs/A__B.parquet, plus views.sql (DuckDB table macros) and manifest.json. The directory and its subfolders are created. A DIRECTORY, not a .tsv file: the PYB-1 extension rule is for options that write one table. mzLib refuses a directory that is not empty, because a snapshot is written once and compared with a later one, never updated in place; the bridge applies that same refusal, and refuses a path that is a file, before any input is read. Absent: nothing is written.}
\item{\code{pair_status} (wire \code{--pair-status})}{string; default absent; range \code{FROM,TO: two different species of the snapshot}. Return pair_status: every gene of FROM's gene set, in ordinal order of gene id, with its status in TO (OrthologySnapshot.PairStatus). Two production names separated by a comma, e.g. homo_sapiens,mus_musculus. Both must be species given on stdin, and different; the bridge checks that before any input is read, with the rule mzLib's PairStatus applies. One direction per call: status is not symmetric. Absent: pair_status is null.}
}
}
\section{Returned fields}{
Each field with its type, its unit, and what \code{NA} means when it is \code{NA}.

\describe{
\item{\code{release}}{string; never \code{NA}. The \code{--release} given (OrthologySnapshot.Release).}
\item{\code{species}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. The snapshot's species, Ensembl production names, in ordinal order (OrthologySnapshot.Species).}
\item{\code{format}}{string; never \code{NA}. ensembl-orthology-snapshot (OrthologySnapshotWriter.FormatName): what manifest.json's format says.}
\item{\code{format_version}}{int; never \code{NA}. 1 at d10017ce (OrthologySnapshotWriter.FormatVersion).}
\item{\code{snapshot_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. OrthologySnapshotWriter.SnapshotId: lower-case hex sha256 over the format, the release, the species and every input's sha256. The same inputs give the same id, written or not; with \code{--out} it is manifest.json's snapshot_id. Record it with any result drawn from the snapshot.}
\item{\code{identical_duplicates_dropped}}{int; in \strong{rows}; never \code{NA}. Homology rows met in both of a pair's dumps, identical in every value, and counted once (OrthologySnapshot.IdenticalDuplicatesDropped). 0 in release 116, whose dumps partition; counted so a change is visible.}
\item{\code{gene_sets}}{map<string,object>; never \code{NA}. Species to its gene set, in species order; each value has genes resolve's gene_set fields (typed under result.tables.gene_sets).}
\item{\code{gene_trees}}{object; never \code{NA}. The gene-tree file and what was kept from it (typed under result.tables.gene_trees).}
\item{\code{dumps}}{map<string,object>; never \code{NA}. Species (the dump's genome, read from its rows) to its homology dump, in species order (typed under result.tables.dumps).}
\item{\code{pairs}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. One entry per species pair (a, b) with a <= b in ordinal order, (s, s) included, in OrthologySnapshot.Pairs order: how many relationships of each class it holds (typed under result.tables.pairs).}
\item{\code{written}}{object; \code{NA} when no \code{--out} was given; nothing was written. The written snapshot: its directory and every file but manifest.json, which sits beside them (typed under result.tables.written).}
\item{\code{pair_status}}{object; \code{NA} when no \code{--pair-status} was given. \{from_species, to_species, row_count, status_counts, column_names, columns\}: one row per gene of from_species (typed under result.tables.pair_status and result.tables.pair_status_columns).}
\item{\code{caveats}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. This call's traps: an input whose file name carries no release (not checked against \code{--release}), dumps sharing one file name (source_dump cannot tell them apart), a GTF named for another release, identical duplicate rows, unrestricted gene trees holding genes outside the gene sets.}
