\section{Fields of \code{pair_status$records}}{
a data.frame, one row per gene.

\describe{
\item{\code{gene_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. Stable Ensembl gene id, unversioned as Ensembl GTFs write it.}
\item{\code{gene_name}}{string; \code{NA} when the GTF gives the gene no gene_name (common for novel lncRNAs). The release's gene_name: a display label, never a key.}
\item{\code{gene_biotype}}{string; never \code{NA}. The release's gene_biotype, unknown when the GTF omits it. Filter on it to choose the denominator: all biotypes are rows, and protein_coding alone is not the same set.}
\item{\code{status}}{string; never \code{NA}. OrthologySnapshot.StatusName: has_ortholog (at least one ortholog in to_species), no_edge_in_shared_tree (the gene's tree holds a to_species gene and Compara called no ortholog to it: the closest the data comes to no ortholog exists), tree_lacks_target_species (the tree holds no to_species gene, so nothing was compared), or not_in_any_tree (in the gene set but in no gene tree: no call was attempted).}
}
}
\section{Fields of \code{parquet_genes}}{
the columns of genes/SPECIES.parquet in a written snapshot; mzLibR does not read Parquet.

\describe{
\item{\code{gene_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. genes/SPECIES.parquet, one row per gene of the gene set.}
\item{\code{gene_version}}{int; \code{NA} when the GTF gives no gene_version (absent is not 0). The gene_version attribute.}
\item{\code{gene_biotype}}{string; never \code{NA}. unknown when the GTF omits it.}
\item{\code{gene_name}}{string; \code{NA} when the GTF gives no gene_name. A display label.}
\item{\code{seq_region}}{string; never \code{NA}. The GTF's first column: chromosome or scaffold.}
\item{\code{species}}{string; never \code{NA}. The production name.}
}
}
\section{Fields of \code{parquet_members}}{
the columns of members/SPECIES.parquet in a written snapshot.

\describe{
\item{\code{gene_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. members/SPECIES.parquet, one row per gene of the gene set that is in a gene tree.}
\item{\code{species}}{string; never \code{NA}. The production name.}
\item{\code{group_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. compara-RELEASE:TREE_ID, so a tree id is never compared across releases.}
\item{\code{source_group_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. Compara's gene-tree stable id, e.g. ENSGT00940000153582.}
\item{\code{canonical_protein_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. The protein Compara used for the gene (the row flagged Y).}
}
}
\section{Fields of \code{parquet_pairs}}{
the columns of pairs/A__B.parquet in a written snapshot.

\describe{
\item{\code{homology_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. pairs/A__B.parquet, one row per relationship, ordered by homology id. Compara's id, unique within a release.}
\item{\code{relationship_type}}{string; never \code{NA}. Compara's homology_type verbatim, e.g. ortholog_one2many.}
\item{\code{relationship_class}}{string; never \code{NA}. ortholog, paralog or homoeolog (ComparaHomologyDump.ClassName).}
\item{\code{species_a}}{string; never \code{NA}. The species that sorts first.}
\item{\code{species_b}}{string; never \code{NA}. The other species.}
\item{\code{gene_a}}{string; never \code{NA}. Side A's gene; every value moves with its gene when a row is reoriented.}
\item{\code{protein_a}}{string; never \code{NA}. Side A's protein in the alignment.}
\item{\code{identity_a}}{float; in \strong{percent}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL. Percentage of A's protein identical to B's in the alignment, 0 to 100.}
\item{\code{gene_b}}{string; never \code{NA}. Side B's gene.}
\item{\code{protein_b}}{string; never \code{NA}. Side B's protein.}
\item{\code{identity_b}}{float; in \strong{percent}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL. Percentage of B's protein identical to A's, 0 to 100. Not identity_a: each is over its own protein's length.}
\item{\code{dn}}{float; in \strong{nonsynonymous substitutions per nonsynonymous site}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL (it computes dN for few comparisons). Compara's dn verbatim.}
\item{\code{ds}}{float; in \strong{synonymous substitutions per synonymous site}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL. Compara's ds verbatim.}
\item{\code{goc_score}}{int; in \strong{score, 0 to 100}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL (not computed for this pair). Gene order conservation score.}
\item{\code{wga_coverage}}{float; in \strong{percent}; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL (no whole-genome alignment for this pair). Whole-genome alignment coverage, 0 to 100.}
\item{\code{is_high_confidence}}{bool; \code{NA} when Compara gave NULL: it declines to say, which is not false. Compara's high-confidence flag.}
\item{\code{source_dump}}{string; never \code{NA}. The FILE NAME of the dump the row was read from. Which of a pair's two dumps holds a row is arbitrary, so this is provenance, not meaning; and as Ensembl names every dump the same, it cannot say which species' dump.}
}
}
\section{Errors}{
Each is an R condition carrying the class shown and \code{mzlib_error}; see \code{\link{mzlib_error}}.

\describe{
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{\code{--release} or \code{--gene-trees} missing or given without a value; \code{--out} or \code{--pair-status} given without a value; a value after \code{--restrict-gene-trees}}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{no species lines on stdin, no line holding only --, or no dump paths after it; a species line that is not species, tab, gtf or gene-set, tab, path; a species listed twice}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{a GTF, gene-set table, dump or gene-trees file that does not exist; \code{--out} naming a file or a directory that is not empty (OrthologySnapshotWriter.WriteAsync's refusal, applied before any input is read)}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{\code{--pair-status} not two comma-separated names, naming a species not given on stdin, or one species twice (the rule of OrthologySnapshot.PairStatus, applied before any input is read)}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{mzLib's OrthologySnapshot.Build refused the inputs as not fitting together (ArgumentException, with mzLib's message): the gene trees or a dump name a release other than \code{--release}; a dump is of a species not given, two dumps are of one species, or a species has no dump}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{a file mzLib's reader refuses (InvalidDataException, naming file and line): a GTF gene row without gene_id; a table that is not a gene-set table; a dump with other columns, a short row, a value that is not a number, an unknown homology_type, a paralog between two species, an ortholog within one, or a second genome; a gene-tree row without four cells or with a flag other than Y or N, or a kept gene in two trees or without exactly one canonical protein}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{OrthologySnapshot.Build found data that breaks the snapshot's rules (InvalidDataException): one gene in two species' gene sets, a homology naming a gene its species' gene set lacks (the gene set and the dumps are from different releases), or one homology id with two different rows}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{the snapshot could not be written (IOException from the disk)}
\item{\code{mzlib_service_unavailable} (service_unavailable)}{Never raised by this verb.}
}
}
\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Every species' homology dump is required, and mzLib refuses a call without one. Compara's README warns that each genome's dump holds an arbitrary subset of its relationships, and in practice they partition: in release 116 every human-mouse orthology is in the mouse dump and none is in the human one, so the dump named after one species gives a complete-looking, badly incomplete answer.
\item Orthology is asserted per species pair and is not transitive: A~B and B~C do not make A~C. Never chain pairs into groups. The written views.sql's species_set3 macro, and mzLib's OrthologySnapshot.SpeciesSet, form a tuple only when every two members are orthologs.
\item Nothing is picked, merged or collapsed: a one-to-many or many-to-many orthology is several rows, and a gene's paralogs are the pair (s, s).
\item A status says how far Compara's inference got, not what biology holds. no_edge_in_shared_tree is the nearest the data comes to no ortholog exists; tree_lacks_target_species and not_in_any_tree mean nothing was compared.
\item Every biotype is in pair_status and in genes/SPECIES.parquet. Which genes form a denominator is the caller's choice: filter on gene_biotype. mzLib's own human-to-mouse check counted protein-coding plus IG and TR genes, which is not the same set as protein_coding alone.
\item Values are Compara's, verbatim: a NULL in a dump stays null in the Parquet (identity, dn, ds, goc_score, wga_coverage, is_high_confidence), never 0 or false. identity_a and identity_b differ because each is over its own protein.
\item The gene set and the dumps must be from one release: mzLib refuses a homology whose gene is not in its species' gene set. Use the primary-assembly GTF (Species.Assembly.Release.gtf.gz), as for genes resolve.
\item A snapshot is written once. The same inputs give the same bytes and the same snapshot_id, so a later release is a new directory compared with the old one; a directory that is not empty is refused, never overwritten.
\item mzLib ships a writer and no reader. Query the Parquet with DuckDB (load views.sql for the orthologs, pair_status and species_set3 macros), pyarrow, polars or R's arrow.
\item Ensembl names every species' dump Compara.RELEASE.protein_default.homologies.tsv.gz in its own folder, and the pairs tables' source_dump column records only the file name, so it cannot tell two species' dumps apart. manifest.json's inputs list each dump with its species and sha256.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
Everything is read into memory, and the inputs are large: human release 116's GTF is 141 MB compressed (mzLib's EnsemblGeneSet remarks), and the vertebrate gene-tree file holds 4.2 million genes unless \code{--restrict-gene-trees} keeps only the given species'. For human, mouse and rat at release 116, mzLib #1381 reports the build from the three dumps at about 20 s and the written snapshot (13 files, 9.0 MB) at about 40 s more. No call here takes a degree of parallelism, so there is no \code{--threads}.
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. 
}
