\item Python (pyMzLib): \code{pymzlib.proteins.build_orthology}
\item Rust (mzLibRust): \code{mzlib::proteins::build_orthology_with} with \code{OrthologyOptions}
\item R (mzLibR): \code{proteins_build_orthology}
}
}
\section{Since}{
Wire protocol 1; pyMzLib 0.6.0; mzLibRust 0.1.0; mzLibR 0.1.0.
}
\section{Not yet verified}{
The spec records these as open. They are listed rather than hidden:

\itemize{
\item result.tables (gene_sets, gene_trees, dumps, pairs, written, pair_status and the Parquet tables) is not validated by tools/check_verbs.py, which checks only envelope_fields, columns and bulk. Bridge tests pin each nested object's keys (GenesOrthologyTests).
\item The Parquet tables' units are mzLib's doc comments (ComparaHomology: identity percent, goc_score and wga_coverage 0 to 100). dn and ds are written as dN and dS conventionally are, substitutions per site; neither mzLib nor this bridge has checked Compara's own definition.
\item Only one pair-status direction per call, and no SpeciesSet tuples or ortholog rows on the wire: the written Parquet and views.sql answer those. Whether a binding needs them without a Parquet reader (pyMzLib has no third-party dependency) is open.
\item source_dump records the dump's file name, which Ensembl gives every species' dump (PUSH-TO-MAINLAND: mzLib could record the dump's genome, or the path under homologies/, beside it).
\item Not yet ported to mzLibRust or mzLibR; the binding names above are proposals.
}
}
